Keep pty write chunks on UTF-8 scalar boundaries.

`Exec.queueWrite` splits every outbound write into 64-byte buffers by raw
byte count. A paste (`ghostty_surface_text` → `completeClipboardPaste`)
whose body is longer than 64 bytes can therefore be cut in the middle of a
multi-byte scalar: 21 CJK characters are 63 bytes, so the 22nd character
lands as `E5` at the end of write #1 and `91 A2` at the start of write #2.
The child usually sees those as two separate read()s, and any TUI that
decodes each chunk on its own (cursor-agent, `chunk.toString()` style
readers) renders U+FFFD × 3 in the middle of the sentence. Apple Terminal,
WezTerm, Alacritty and kitty write the paste body contiguously; iTerm2 and
VS Code chunk by character, never by byte.

Resolve the chunk end with `utf8ChunkEnd`: when the first byte of the next
chunk is a continuation byte inside a valid scalar, hand the lead byte
(and any continuation bytes already copied) to the next write. Both the
fast path and the linefeed (`\r` → `\r\n`) path use it; the bytes given back
are never `\r` so they map 1:1 onto the buffer tail. Malformed input or a
chunk that would become empty falls back to the byte boundary so the
writer can never stall. Buffer size stays 64.

